{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom at b0947a29 on request/commitment-honesty. Successor to ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc.\n\nCloses the four blockers from codex's review of a668051e.\n\nOne word covered two events that end differently. A third party retiring the request ends the work; a third party retiring only the promise leaves the request live and able to take a replacement. Both were filed as one terminal status, and terminal rows are dropped from the bounded lists entirely — so an authority removing someone else's promise became the quietest thing on the board, quieter than the reneged row it replaced. A retired request now reads revoked and a retired promise reads released, and neither is terminal, because a third-party retirement is exactly the row a reader needs to see. I had named this risk when I asked for the review and shipped it anyway; codex's argument for why it mattered was sharper than my hedge.\n\nThe fold profile was left at workroom-fold@2 while the fold's output changed. A resident holding a checkpoint written by the old fold would have reused it and continued under the new executable, so what a reader saw would depend on which checkpoints that process happened to hold, and two residents on one log would disagree without saying why. The profile is now @3, pinned by a test that fails if the contract moves again and the marker does not.\n\nThe canonical account described the old fold. docs/concepts/work-loop.md claimed eight statuses and said a ratifier's retirement produced withdrawn, cancelled or reneged; it now carries ten, both diagrams, who causes each, and why satisfaction is terminal while released is not. docs/reference/gs/status.md named the attention lane without the new states, and docs/reference/mcp/status.md listed the categories that never discharge without them — that last page has no gate watching it and I found it only by sweeping every file that mentions a status word.\n\nThe served bundle was the predecessor's, so the resident would have served UI with no word for either status. make ui-check passes at this head.\n\nretirementActors became retiredByAnother and returns a boolean per event rather than actor slices, since the target's author is known where the comparison happens.\n\nBoth original fixes remain and stay mutation-checked: ignoring the superseding actor reproduces the blame misattribution, and restoring the old ordering reproduces the erased satisfaction that five commitments in this workroom are still showing.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc, the browser tests and ui-check all pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nMerging this must follow the resident-rebuild progress work, so a fold profile bump arrives with honest rebuild reporting, as hugh directed.","body":{"branch":"request/commitment-honesty","commit":"b0947a29","head":"b0947a29","path":"internal/workroom"}}